From 9c10c892b3c7c8a59c34f5a8c3bf2d89697022db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 05:20:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/20] relay_metrics: Count tunneled directory response bytes
 by request type

Under a BEGIN_DIR flood, an operator needs to know what the relay spends
its bandwidth on. Stock tor counts directory bytes only in total, and
only for its daily history; the traffic counters can't tell directory
responses from relayed traffic.

Count the response bytes that each tunneled directory connection's
stream reads, where tor already counts directory bytes, by the request
type that the connection keeps, as
tor_relay_begindir_response_bytes_total{type}. Every request we answer
is typed before its answer. This counts work done, not bytes the client
received; like stock tor's directory byte history, it misses the bytes
still queued when the directory connection closes, which
conn_close_if_marked() hands to the stream unread. An onion service
descriptor that isn't found is a short 404, so "hs_get" bytes are
almost all descriptors served.

Part of #41339.
